votes.reply_rewrite:
Thanks for working through it. Your second reading is right on the main point. On main, `try_make_unique_dma_for_overwrite` called `dma_allocate_aligned` directly. The accessors then cast the bytes with a plain `reinterpret_cast`. No `kv_block` ever started its lifetime. A member access through such a pointer is undefined behaviour (N4950, the C++23 draft standard, [basic.life]/6). GCC 14.3 with -Wall -Wextra gives no warning on a small sample of that pattern, and ASan/UBSan run it clean.

Two details, so the Note states the rule exactly:

- `operator new` is one item on the list, not the only one. [intro.object]/13 and its note also list `malloc` and its relatives, `memcpy`/`memmove`, `bit_cast`, and starting the lifetime of a `std::byte` array. `dma_allocate_aligned` is on none of these lists. That is why the allocation now goes through a function named `operator new[]`.
- `v_vnni_tensor` needs the same care as the old `kv_block`, not more. Both are implicit-lifetime types (types that an allocation may create without a constructor call, [class.prop]/9). The old `kv_block` was an aggregate (public members, no constructors) of arrays. The new one still is. `v_vnni_tensor` has a trivial implicit default constructor and a trivial destructor. The `static_assert` inside `kv_block` and the two in the accessors keep that true. So the rule was the same before and after. What changed is that the allocation now satisfies it.

I reworded the Note in <commit id> (comment only, no code change). It now says what the KV book does, what an implicit-lifetime type is, which operations create such objects, why `dma_allocate_aligned` is not one of them, what goes wrong without the wrapper, and why the unit tests cannot tell the difference (`aligned_alloc` is on the list, [c.malloc]/4). The title and the facts of the old paragraphs stay. Does the new text read clearly to you?

---
Rewritten Note (replaces memory.hpp:167-183; 30 lines, all at or under 80 columns):

// Note [DMA allocation creates objects]
// ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
// The KV book (struct book in tron/models/kv_cache.hpp) reads its arenas as
// arrays of kv_block objects that no code ever constructs. C++ allows that
// only for implicit-lifetime types: types that an allocation may create
// without a constructor call. In short, these are scalars, arrays, and
// classes with a trivial constructor and destructor ([basic.types.general],
// [class.prop]). Such objects come into existence only through a short list
// of operations: a call of a function named operator new or operator new[],
// std::malloc and its relatives, std::memcpy and std::memmove, std::bit_cast,
// and starting the lifetime of an unsigned char or std::byte array
// ([intro.object]). Each of them creates, in the memory it hands back or
// fills, the objects that the program goes on to use.
//
// dma_allocate_aligned is not on that list. It is an ordinary function, so
// the memory it returns holds no objects. A cast of that memory to kv_block*
// creates none either. A member access through such a pointer is undefined
// behaviour ([basic.life]). Compiler warnings and sanitizers do not catch it.
// So try_make_unique_dma_for_overwrite gets its memory through the wrapper
// below, a function named operator new[]. That call creates the objects. The
// wrapper writes no bytes and runs no constructor.
//
// Call the wrapper directly, never from a new-expression. An array
// new-expression may ask for extra bytes ([expr.new]), and dma_deallocate would
// not free them.
//
// No test fails if a caller goes back to calling dma_allocate_aligned directly.
// The unit-test allocator (aligned_alloc in src/pos/fake.cpp) is on the list
// above ([c.malloc]). So it creates the objects by itself, and the tests behave
// the same either way.

---
Follow-up issue (rewritten):

Title: DMA factories other than try_make_unique_dma_for_overwrite create no objects for trivial element types

## Short version

PR #4557 routes `try_make_unique_dma_for_overwrite` through a function named `operator new[]`. That call makes the objects the caller reads exist in the returned memory (Note [DMA allocation creates objects], h/system/memory.hpp). The other three DMA factories still call `dma_allocate_aligned` directly, and for trivially constructible element types they run no placement new. In strict C++ their memory then holds no objects.

## Where

At PR #4557 head c73e7fb2f9, h/system/memory.hpp:

- `make_unique_dma<T>` (:239-241): `dma_allocate_aligned`, then `std::memset` when `T` is trivially constructible.
- `make_unique_dma<T[]>` (:254-257): same, with `std::memset` over the array.
- `make_unique_dma_for_overwrite<T>` (:274-275): `dma_allocate_aligned`, placement new only when `T` is not trivially constructible.
- `make_unique_dma_for_overwrite<T[]>` (:286-289): same for arrays.

`std::memset` is not one of the operations that implicitly create objects (the C++ rule that certain calls make objects exist in the memory they return). N4950 (the C++23 draft standard) [intro.object]/13 and its note list `operator new`/`operator new[]`, `malloc` and relatives ([c.malloc]), `memcpy`/`memmove` ([cstring.syn]), `bit_cast` ([bit.cast]) and `start_lifetime_as` ([obj.lifetime]). A member access on the result is undefined behaviour ([basic.life]/6).

## Why it matters

Compiler warnings and sanitizers do not catch this (GCC 14.3 -Wall -Wextra and ASan/UBSan are silent on a small sample). The unit-test allocator (`aligned_alloc` in src/pos/fake.cpp:354) creates objects by itself ([c.malloc]/4), so no unit test can show it. The production DMA allocator gives no such guarantee. The maintainer review of PR #4557 (comment 4133869073) called the same gap in the KV book strictly incorrect.

## Proposed fix

Route all four factories through the tagged `operator new[]` wrapper that PR #4557 added. The wrapper writes no bytes, so behaviour and performance stay the same. Keep the `TRON_ASSERT` on null in the asserting forms (memory.hpp:255, :287).

## Out of scope

The non-ISO reads tracked in #4588 (`scaled_v_expr`, `fill_storage_slot`, the 8-lane V accessors) are a separate gap.

Label: Tech Debt.

